# Build context for packages/ludo_server/Dockerfile, built from the
# repository root because the pub workspace root has to be present (see the
# Dockerfile's own comment on that). Everything below never needs to reach
# the daemon: it is either irrelevant to compiling the server, or, for the
# secret-shaped patterns, must never reach a place a build cache or an image
# layer could retain it even after a later step deletes it.

# Version control and CI. The daemon does not need history and this is not
# CI's file to touch.
.git/
.github/

# Dart build/tool caches. Rebuilt fresh in the build stage, and shipping a
# host machine's .dart_tool would poison the workspace resolution done
# inside the container with paths that do not exist there.
.dart_tool/
**/.dart_tool/
.packages
**/.packages
build/
**/build/
.pub-cache/
.pub/
pubspec.lock.bak

# Secrets and key material. Mirrors .gitignore's secret block; a build
# context is not a commit, but the same rule applies for the same reason --
# a file deleted in a later Dockerfile layer is still in the image, and a
# file never sent to the daemon cannot end up in a layer at all.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.jks
*.keystore
*.p12
*.pem
*.key
key.properties
keystore.properties
service-account*.json
play-service-account*.json
google-services.json
secrets/
*.secret

# Everything else that has no business in a server image build.
docs/
work/
scratch/
tmp/
*.log
coverage/
doc/api/
.idea/
.vscode/
node_modules/
android/
ios/

# This deploy directory is deploy-time material for the host, not build
# input for the image.
deploy/

# Do not send the Dockerfile-adjacent tooling files themselves twice; they
# are already read directly by the docker build invocation.
.dockerignore
